<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oneida</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Onʌyotaʼá꞉ka, People of the Standing Stone</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Oneida, People of the Standing Stone, are an Indigenous people of the Northeastern Woodlands and one of the five founding nations of the Haudenosaunee Confederacy, traditionally living between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onondaga-people">Onondaga</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oneida-lake">Oneida Lake</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. In the early 17th century they held about 6 million acres. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution">American Revolution</a>, after the Confederacy's neutrality broke down, most Oneida sided with the revolutionaries, unlike most of the other nations, and about fifty fought at Oriskany in 1777. After the war New York reduced their land drastically, and many moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wisconsin">Wisconsin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>; today there are Oneida nations in New York, Wisconsin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oneida-people.jpg" alt="Oneida" width="330" height="428" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Catlin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bread,_Chief_of_the_Tribe.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Together they persuaded the Mohawk, <b>Oneida</b>, Onondaga, Cayuga and Seneca to accept the Great Law of Peace, the Gayanashagowa, and finally won over the feared Onondaga chief Tadodaho, whose name became the title of the league’s presiding chief.</blockquote><a href="/empire/iroquois-confederacy">Iroquois Confederacy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Jacques Bruyas’s Mohawk dictionary defines gatsennen as “domestic animal, servant, slave”, and <b>Oneida</b> and Onondaga words likewise compared slaves to dogs.</blockquote><a href="/societies/haudenosaunee">Social Hierarchy of the Haudenosaunee</a></li><li><blockquote>Some versions heard before 1860 tell of a trader who wished to marry the daughter of Shenandoah (1710–1816), a chief of the <b>Oneida</b> Iroquois of Oneida Castle in central New York, who helped found the Oneida Academy that became Hamilton College and is buried there.</blockquote><a href="/songs/oh-shenandoah">Oh Shenandoah</a></li></ul></section>
